# The generic-args ANCHOR-ADVANCE pin — a POS / idempotence pin, NOT a
# red-verified regression fixture. Read the disposition before trusting
# the name (Core #12: a fixture's name is a claim about scope).
#
# WHAT IT PINS: explicit generic arguments can contain a `(` of their own —
# `Callable[void(int)]` does — so the argument-tuple gate must anchor at
# the generic-args `]`, never at the callee or method name. A scan started
# at the name end would return the `(` INSIDE the brackets and gate over a
# window that begins mid-generic-args. Both callee shapes appear: a free
# function (`Expr::Call`) and a method (`Expr::MethodCall`).
#
# WHY THERE IS NO RED HERE: the two anchor choices emit IDENTICAL output on
# every SOURCE-REACHABLE shape — measured, not assumed. Neither line here
# carries an interior comment, so the gate does not fire either way, and a
# comment cannot be placed inside `Callable[void(int)]`'s parens without
# the line comment swallowing the rest of the line. What this fixture
# therefore pins is that the `]`-scan premise on `Type::Named` spans holds
# and that these shapes stay formattable, re-parseable and idempotent.
#
# The class's actual net is the BREAK-AND-VERIFY procedure recorded in the
# round's report: force the generic-args gate to miss, run a
# generics-interior-comment shape, observe the comment hoovered into the
# argument list, restore.

void identity[T](T c):
    print(1)

struct P2:
    int n

equip P2:
    void pick[T](self, T c):
        print(2)

void cb(int x):
    print(x)

int main():
    auto c = cb
    identity[Callable[void(int)]](c)
    P2 b = P2(1)
    b.pick[Callable[void(int)]](c)
    return 0
